  "id": "Compound::DB00543 - Gene::1565",
  "verbalization": "Amoxapine is linked to Cyp2d6 through a series of molecular and pharmacological interactions. The evidence suggests that Amoxapine causes Adenitis or Parotid Swelling, which are both mediated by Protriptyline or Trimipramine, both of which bind to Cyp2d6. The lowest common ancestors of these pathways—Tricyclic Antidepressants and Organic Tricyclic Compounds—highlight a shared pharmacological profile, reinforcing the role of Cyp2d6 in the metabolism of these compounds. The consistent binding of Protriptyline and Trimipramine to Cyp2d6, along with the causal relationships established by Amoxapine, indicates that Cyp2d6 is a key enzyme in the metabolic pathway of these drugs. The integration of these pathways suggests that Amoxapine may influence Cyp2d6 activity through the activation of Tricyclic Antidepressants, which in turn modulate Cyp2d6 function. This mechanism underscores the importance of Cyp2d6 in the pharmacological effects of Amoxapine and related compounds.",
